---
/**
 * /adult-drivers-ed/texas/foreign-license-texas — a license from another country.
 *
 * Rebuilt 2026-10-13 on the hook → answer → loop → funnel recipe. §521.1601(b) exempts only a license issued by
 * another state; DPS adds Canada, U.S. territories and five reciprocity countries. Everyone else is a new applicant,
 * and at 18 through 24 the course applies, so the page ends in the course band. The page does not state the exam
 * language, so the band heading does not either.
 */
import AdeGuide from '../../../components/ade/AdeGuide.astro';
import { law, reciprocityCountries, newResident, fees, itd } from '../../../data/ade-eligibility';

const base = '/adult-drivers-ed/texas';
const url = `${base}/foreign-license-texas`;
const recip = reciprocityCountries;
const g = newResident.graceDays;

// The one-breath answer: meta description and the first sentence of the lede.
const answer = `Texas can waive the knowledge and driving tests for a foreign license only from Canada, a U.S. territory or five reciprocity countries, per DPS. Everyone else takes both, plus the six-hour course at ${law.requiredBand}.`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can I trade my foreign driver license for a Texas one?',
    a: `Only if it was issued by a U.S. territory, Canada, or one of the five countries Texas holds reciprocity with: ${recip.join(', ')}. Every other foreign license has to go through the full Texas process: application, vision test, knowledge test and driving skills test, plus the six-hour adult driver education course if you are 18 through 24.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'I have driven for ten years in my home country. Do I really need a beginner course?',
    a: 'Yes, if you are 18 through 24 and your license was not issued by a U.S. state, a U.S. territory, Canada or a reciprocity country. Texas Transportation Code §521.1601 ties the requirement to age and to holding a license issued by another state, not to how long you have been driving, so it catches experienced drivers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long can I drive in Texas on my foreign license?',
    a: `Up to ${g} days after moving to Texas. DPS says you can legally drive on a valid, unexpired license from another U.S. state, U.S. territory, Canadian province or qualifying country for that long. After that you need a Texas license.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my license need to be translated?',
    a: `Yes, if it is not in English or Spanish. DPS requires the translation to be done by a translation service or your consulate before you arrive in Texas, so arrange it before you travel rather than after.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'I am on a student or work visa. Can I get a Texas license?',
    a: `Yes. Texas issues a limited term driver license to temporary visitors who can show lawful presence. It costs $${fees.limitedTermVisitor} and expires when your period of lawful presence expires, or after one year if your status is recorded as "duration of status". The driver education rule works the same way: required if you are 18 through 24 and this is your first U.S.-state license.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Am I exempt at 25 or older?',
    a: 'From the course, yes. DPS states there are no driver education requirements at 25 and older. You would still take the knowledge and skills exams, since a non-reciprocity foreign license does not waive them, and you would still need an Impact Texas Adult Drivers certificate before the skills test.',
  },
];

const next = [
  { href: `${base}/temporary-visitors-and-students`, title: 'On a student or work visa', blurb: 'The limited term license, lawful presence and the timing traps students hit.' },
  { href: `${base}/do-i-need-adult-drivers-ed`, title: 'Do you need the course?', blurb: 'The Texas rule by age and by the license you already hold.' },
  { href: `${base}/texas-driver-license-documents`, title: 'What to bring to the appointment', blurb: 'Identity, lawful presence, Social Security number and two printed proofs of residency.' },
  { href: `${base}/cost`, title: 'What the course costs', blurb: 'The price, and what it includes.' },
];
---

<AdeGuide
  title={`Foreign Driver License in Texas: Only 5 Countries Transfer, ${g} Days to Drive on It | Road Ready Safety`}
  description={answer}
  url={url}
  eyebrow="New residents"
  h1="Foreign license in Texas? Only five countries and Canada get a transfer. Everyone else starts over"
  lede={`${answer} You can drive on a valid license from a qualifying country for up to ${g} days after you move. The five countries are listed below, and the list leaves out most of the places people move to Texas from.`}
  faqs={faqs}
  dateModified="2026-10-13"
  updated="October 13, 2026"
  band={{ eyebrow: 'Most countries are not reciprocal', heading: 'Licensed abroad and 18 to 24? The course applies, and its exam is in the price' }}
  next={next}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Tex. Transp. Code §521.1601, Driver Education Required; Exception', href: 'https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/TN/htm/TN.521.htm' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Moving to Texas: A Guide to Driver Licenses and IDs', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/moving-texas' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Apply for a Texas Driver License', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/apply-texas-driver-license' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Driver License Fees', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/driver-license-fees' },
  ]}
>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Does a foreign license exempt you from Texas drivers ed?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Usually not. Texas Transportation Code §521.1601 requires driver education before the state will license
      anyone under {law.ageCeiling}, and subsection (b), the entire exemption, covers only a license from
      another state:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-border pl-5 py-1 m-0">
      <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px] italic m-0">&ldquo;{law.exemptionText}&rdquo;</p>
      <cite class="text-sm text-text-muted not-italic">&mdash; Tex. Transp. Code §521.1601(b)</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      <strong>Another state,</strong> not another country. A{' '}
      <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={`${base}/new-resident-out-of-state-license`}>license from Louisiana</a>{' '}
      exempts a 23-year-old; a license from Honduras does not. DPS applies the statute the same way, and a lot of experienced adult
      drivers arrive in Texas to find they are treated as beginners.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Which foreign licenses can you transfer to Texas?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Canada, U.S. territories and five reciprocity countries. DPS is slightly more generous than the bare
      statute. From its Moving to Texas page:
    </p>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3 pl-5 m-0 list-disc marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>A U.S. state, a U.S. territory, or Canada.</strong> Surrender the license and you are
        not required to take the knowledge or skills exams.
      </li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Reciprocity countries: {recip.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and {recip.at(-1)}.</strong>{' '}
        A valid, unexpired license from one of these five may waive the knowledge and skills exams,
        unless you choose to keep your out-of-country license.
      </li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Everywhere else.</strong> Full process, and the six-hour adult driver education
        course if you are {law.requiredBand}. The steps in order are in{' '}
        <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={`${base}/how-to-get-a-texas-license-as-an-adult`}>how to get a Texas driver license as an adult</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Five countries is a short list. Mexico is not on it. Neither are India, the Philippines, Vietnam, China,
      Nigeria, Brazil, El Salvador, Guatemala or Honduras, which between them account for most of the people
      who move to Texas with a license already in their pocket.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">How long can you drive in Texas on a foreign license?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Up to {g} days after you move. DPS lets you drive on a valid, unexpired license from another U.S. state,
      U.S. territory, Canadian province or qualifying country for that long. That is the window to get
      everything else done.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What should you do before you arrive?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Get the license translated if it is not in English or Spanish, and book the DPS appointment early.
    </p>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3 pl-5 m-0 list-disc marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Get the license translated.</strong> DPS: &ldquo;{newResident.translationRule}&rdquo;
        A translation service or your consulate can do it; the counter will not.
      </li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Book the appointment early.</strong> DPS driver license offices are appointment only,
        and the driving skills test is the slowest thing to get. See{' '}
        <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={`${base}/impact-texas-adult-drivers`}>how the queue interacts with the {itd.certValidDays}-day ITD certificate</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Can you get a Texas license on a student or work visa?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Yes, a limited term driver license, if you can document lawful presence. The fee is
      ${fees.limitedTermVisitor}, and it expires when your period of lawful presence expires, or after one year
      if your status is recorded as &ldquo;duration of status&rdquo;. The driver education question is
      unchanged: it depends on your age and on who issued any license you already hold. More in{' '}
      <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={`${base}/temporary-visitors-and-students`}>Texas licenses for visitors and students</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

</AdeGuide>
